" (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1112.0510)\nciteturn245view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1112.0510\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 11095\nL0@P0: arXiv:1112.0510v1 [math.PR] 2 Dec 2011\nL1@P0: SIMPLY GENERATED TREES, CONDITIONED\nL2@P0: GALTON–WATSON TREES, RANDOM ALLOCATIONS\nL3@P0: AND CONDENSATION.\nL4@P0: SVANTE JANSON\nL5@P0: Abstract. We give a unified treatment of the limit, as the size tends\nL6@P0: to infinity, of simply generated random trees, including both the well\u0002known result in the standard case of critical Galton–Watson trees and\nL7@P0: similar but less well-known results in the other cases (i.e., when no\nL8@P0: equivalent critical Galton–Watson tree exists). There is a well-defined\nL9@P0: limit in the form of an infinite random tree in all cases; for critical\nL10@P0: Galton–Watson trees this tree is locally finite but for the other cases the\nL11@P0: random limit has exactly one node of infinite degree.\nL12@P0: The proofs use a well-known connection to a random allocation model\nL13@P0: that we call balls-in-boxes, and we prove corresponding theorems for this\nL14@P0: model.\nL15@P0: This survey paper contains many known results from many different\nL16@P0: sources, together with some new results.\nL17@P0: Contents\nL18@P0: 1. Introduction 2\nL19@P0: 2. Simply generated trees 4\nL20@P0: 2.1. Ordered rooted trees 4\nL21@P0: 2.2. Galton–Watson trees 5\nL22@P0: 2.3. Simply generated trees 6\nL23@P0: 3. Notation 7\nL24@P0: 3.1. More notation 9\nL25@P0: 4. Equivalent weights 9\nL26@P0: 5. A modified Galton–Watson tree 12\nL27@P0: 6. The Ulam–Harris tree and convergence 17\nL28@P0: 7. Main result for simply generated random trees 19\nL29@P0: 8. Three different types of weights 23\nL30@P0: 9. Examples of simply generated random trees 25\nL31@P0: 10. Balls-in-boxes 32\nL32@P0: 11. Examples of balls-in-boxes 36\nL33@P0: 12. Preliminaries 48\nL34@P0: 13. Proofs of Theorems 10.4–10.7 52\nL35@P0: 14. Trees and balls-in-boxes 61\nL36@P0: Date: 2 December 2011.\nL37@P0: 2000 Mathematics Subject Classification. 60C50; 05C05, 60F05, 60J80.\nL38@P0-1: 12 SVANTE JANSON\nL39@P1: 15. Proof of Theorem 7.1 67\nL40@P1: 16. Proofs of Theorems 7.11 and 7.12 71\nL41@P1: 17. Asymptotics of the partition functions 73\nL42@P1: 18. Largest degrees and boxes 83\nL43@P1: 18.1. The case λ 6 ν 84\nL44@P1: 18.2. The subcase σ\nL45@P1: 2 < ∞ 86\nL46@P1: 18.3. The subcase λ < ν 98\nL47@P1: 18.4. The subcase wk+1/wk → 0 as k → ∞ 103\nL48@P1: 18.5. The subcase λ = ν and σ\nL49@P1: 2 = ∞ 106\nL50@P1: 18.6. The case λ > ν 112\nL51@P1: 18.7. Applications to random forests 122\nL52@P1: 19. Large nodes in simply generated trees with ν < 1 128\nL53@P1: 20. Further results and problems 133\nL54@P1: 20.1. Level widths 133\nL55@P1: 20.2. Asymptotic normality 134\nL56@P1: 20.3. Height and width 135\nL57@P1: 20.4. Scaled trees 136\nL58@P1: 20.5. Random walks 136\nL59@P1: 20.6. Multi-type conditioned Galton–Watson trees 137\nL60@P1: 21. Different conditionings for Galton–Watson trees 137\nL61@P1: 21.1. Conditioning on |T | > n. 137\nL62@P1: 21.2. Conditioning on H(T ) > n. 138\nL63@P1: References 140\nL64@P1: 1. Introduction\nL65@P1: The main purpose of this survey paper is to study the asymptotic shape\nL66@P1: of simply generated random trees in complete generality; this includes con\u0002ditioned Galton–Watson trees as a special case, but we will also go beyond\nL67@P1: that case. Definitions are given in Section 2; here we only recall that simply\nL68@P1: generated trees are defined by a weight sequence (wk), and that the case\nL69@P1: when the weight sequence is a probability distribution yields conditioned\nL70@P1: Galton–Watson trees.\nL71@P1: It is well-known that in the case of a critical conditioned Galton–Watson\nL72@P1: tree, i.e., when the defining offspring distribution has expectation 1, the\nL73@P1: random tree has a limit (as the size tends to infinity); this limit is an in\u0002finite random tree, the size-biased Galton–Watson tree defined by Kesten\nL74@P1: [74], see also Aldous [4], Aldous and Pitman [6] and Lyons, Pemantle and\nL75@P1: Peres [84]. It is also well-known that this case is less special than it might\nL76@P1: seem; there is a notion of equivalent weight sequences defining the same\nL77@P1: simply generated random tree, see Section 4, and a large class of weight\nL78@P1: sequences have an equivalent probability weight sequence defining a critical\nL79@P1-2: conditioned Galton–Watson tree. Many probabilists, including myself, haveSIMPLY GENERATED TREES AND RANDOM ALLOCATIONS 3\nL80@P2: often concentrated on this “standard” case of critical conditioned Galton–\nL81@P2: Watson trees and dismissed the remaining cases as uninteresting exceptional\nL82@P2: cases. However, some researchers, in particular mathematical physicists,\nL83@P2: have studied such cases too. Bialas and Burda [13] studied one case (Exam\u0002ple 9.7 below) and found a phase transition as we leave the standard case;\nL84@P2: this can be interpreted as a condensation making the tree bushy with one or\nL85@P2: a few nodes of very high degree. This interesting condensation was studied\nL86@P2: further by Jonsson and Stef´ansson [67], who showed that (in the power-law\nL87@P2: case), there is a limit tree of a different type, having one node of infinite\nL88@P2: degree.\nL89@P2: We give in the present paper a unified treatment of the limit as the size\nL90@P2: tends to infinity for all simply generated trees, including both the well\u0002known result in the standard case of critical Galton–Watson trees and the\nL91@P2: “exceptional” cases (i.e., when no equivalent probability weight sequence\nL92@P2: exists, or when such a sequence exists but not with mean 1). We will see\nL93@P2: that there is a well-defined limit in the form of an infinite random tree for\nL94@P2: any weight sequence. In the non-standard cases, this infinite random limit\nL95@P2: has exactly one node of infinite degree, so its form differs from the standard\nL96@P2: case of a critical Galton–Watson tree where all nodes in the limit tree have\nL97@P2: finite degrees, but nevertheless the trees are similar; see Sections 5 and 7 for\nL98@P2: details.\nL99@P2: Some important notation, used throughout the paper, is introduced in\nL100@P2: Section 3, while Sections 4 and 6 contain further preliminaries. The main\nL101@P2: limit theorem for simply generated random trees is stated in Section 7, to\u0002gether with some other, related, limit theorems concerning node degrees and\nL102@P2: fringe subtrees. The differences between different types of weight sequences\nL103@P2: are discussed further in Section 8.\nL104@P2: The proofs of the limit theorems for random trees use a well-known con\u0002nection to a random allocation model that we call balls-in-boxes; this model\nL105@P2: exhibits a similar behaviour, with condensation in the non-classical cases,\nL106@P2: see e.g. Bialas, Burda and Johnston [14]. The model is defined in Section 10,\nL107@P2: and the relation between the models is described in Section 14. The balls-in\u0002boxes model is interesting in its own right, and it has been used for several\nL108@P2: other applications; we give some examples from probability theory, com\u0002binatorics and statistical physics in Section 11. We therefore also develop\nL109@P2: the general theory for balls-in-boxes with arbitrary weight sequences (in the\nL110@P2: range where the mean occupancy is bounded). In particular, we give in Sec\u0002tion 10 theorems corresponding to (and in some ways extending) our main\nL111@P2: theorems for random trees.\nL112@P2: The limit theorems for balls-in-boxes are proved in Sections 12–13, and\nL113@P2: then these results are used to prove the limit theorems for random trees in\nL114@P2: Sections 14–16.\nL115@P2: The remaining sections contain additional results. Section 17 gives as\u0002ymptotic results for the partition functions of the models. The very long\nL116@P2-3: Section 18 gives results on the largest degrees in random trees, and the4 SVANTE JANSON\nL117@P3: largest numbers of balls in a box in the balls-in-boxes model; the section\nL118@P3: is long because there are several different cases with different types of be\u0002haviour. In particular, we study in Section 18.6 the case when there is\nL119@P3: condensation, and investigate whether this appears as condensation to a\nL120@P3: single box (or node), or whether the condensation is distributed over several\nL121@P3: boxes (nodes); it turns out that both cases can occur. We give also, in Sec\u0002tion 18.7, applications to the size of the largest tree in random forests. In\nL122@P3: Section 19, the condensation in random trees is discussed in further detail.\nL123@P3: Finally, some additional comments, results and open problems are given in\nL124@P3: Sections 20 and 21; Section 20 mentions briefly various other types of as\u0002ymptotic results for simply generated random trees, and Section 21 discusses\nL125@P3: alternative ways to condition Galton–Watson trees.\nL126@P3: This paper contains many known results from many different sources,\nL127@P3: together with some new results. (We believe, for example, that the theorems\nL128@P3: in Section 7 are new in the present generality.) We have tried to give relevant\nL129@P3: references, but the absence of references does not necessarily imply that a\nL130@P3: result is new.\nL131@P3: 2. Simply generated trees\nL132@P3: 2.1. Ordered rooted trees. The trees that we consider are (with a few ex\u0002plicit exceptions) rooted and ordered (such trees are also called plane trees).\nL133@P3: Recall that a tree is rooted if one node is distinguished as the root o; this im\u0002plies that we can arrange the nodes in a sequence of generations (or levels),\nL134@P3: where generation x consists of all nodes of distance x to the root. (Thus\nL135@P3: generation 0 is the root; generation 1 is the set of neighbours of the root,\nL136@P3: and so on.) If v is a node with v 6= o, then the parent of v is the neighbour\nL137@P3: of v on the path from v to o; thus, every node except the root has a unique\nL138@P3: parent, while the root has no parent. Conversely, for any node v, the neigh\u0002bours of v that are further away from the root than v are the children of v.\nL139@P3: The number of children of v is the outdegree d\nL140@P3: +(v) > 0 of v. Note that if v\nL141@P3: is in generation x, then its parent is in generation x − 1 and its children are\nL142@P3: in generation x + 1.\nL143@P3: Recall further that a rooted tree is ordered if the children of each node are\nL144@P3: ordered in a sequence v1, . . . , vd, where d = d\nL145@P3: +(v) > 0 is the outdegree of v.\nL146@P3: See e.g. Drmota [33] for more information on these and other types of trees.\nL147@P3: (The trees we consider are called planted plane trees in [33].) We identify\nL148@P3: trees that are isomorphic in the obvious (order preserving) way. (Formally,\nL149@P3: we can define our trees as equivalence classes. Alternatively, we may select\nL150@P3: a specific representative in each equivalence class as in Section 6.)\nL151@P3: Remark 2.1. Some authors prefer to add an extra (phantom) node as a\nL152@P3: parent of the root; such trees are called planted. (An alternative version is\nL153@P3: to add only a pendant edge at the root, with no second endpoint.) There\nL154@P3: is an obvious one-to-one correspondence between trees with and without\nL155@P3-4: the extra node, so the difference is just a matter of formulations, but whenSIMPLY GENERATED TREES AND RANDOM ALLOCATIONS 5\nL156@P4: comparing results one should be careful whether, for example, the extra\nL157@P4: node is counted or not. The extra node yields the technical advantage that\nL158@P4: also the root has indegree 1 and thus total degree = 1 + d\nL159@P4: +(v); it further\nL160@P4: gives each embedding in the plane a unique ordering of the children of every\nL161@P4: node (in clockwise order from the parent, say). Nevertheless, we find this\nL162@P4: device less natural and we will not use it in the present paper. (We use\nL163@P4: outdegrees instead of degrees and assume that an ordering of the children\nL164@P4: as above is given; then there are no problems.)\nL165@P4: We are primarily interested in (large) finite trees, but we will also con\u0002sider infinite trees, for example as limit objects in our main theorem (Theo\u0002rem 7.1). The infinite trees may have nodes with infinite outdegree d\nL166@P4: +(v) =\nL167@P4: ∞; in this case we assume that the children are ordered v1, v2, . . . (i.e., the\nL168@P4: order type of the set of children is N).\nL169@P4: We let Tn be the set of all ordered rooted trees with n nodes (including\nL170@P4: the root) and let Tf:= S∞\nL171@P4: n=1 Tn be the set of all finite ordered rooted trees;\nL172@P4: see further Section 6.\nL173@P4: Remark 2.2. Note that Tn is a finite set. In fact, it is well-known that its\nL174@P4: size |Tn| is the (n − 1):th Catalan number\nL175@P4: Cn−1 =\nL176@P4: 1\nL177@P4: n\nL178@P4: \u0012\nL179@P4: 2n − 2\nL180@P4: n − 1\nL181@P4: \u0013\nL182@P4: =\nL183@P4: (2n − 2)!\nL184@P4: n! (n − 1)!, (2.1)\nL185@P4: see e.g. [33, Section 1.2.2 and Theorem 3.2], [40, Section I.2.3] or [103,\nL186@P4: Exercise 6.19(e)], but we do not need this.\nL187@P4: For any tree T, we let |T| denote the number of nodes; we call |T| the\nL188@P4: size of T. As is well known, for any finite tree T, X\nL189@P4: v∈T\nL190@P4: d\nL191@P4: +(v) = |T| − 1, (2.2)\nL192@P4: since every node except the root is the child of exactly one node.\nL193@P4: 2.2. Galton–Watson trees. An important class of examples of random\nL194@P4: ordered rooted trees is given by the Galton–Watson trees. These are de\u0002fined as the family trees of Galton–Watson processes: Given a probability\nL195@P4: distribution (πk)∞\nL196@P4: k=0 on Z>0, or, equivalently, a random variable ξ with dis\u0002tribution (πk)∞\nL197@P4: k=0, we build the tree T recursively, starting with the root\nL198@P4: and giving each node a number of children that is an independent copy\nL199@P4: of ξ. (We call (πk)∞\nL200@P4: k=0 the offspring distribution of T ; we sometimes also\nL201@P4: abuse the language and call ξ the offspring distribution.) In other words,\nL202@P4: the outdegrees d\nL203@P4: +(v) are i.i.d. with the distribution (πk)∞\nL204@P4: k=0.\nL205@P4: Recall that the Galton–Watson process is called subcritical, critical or\nL206@P4: supercritical as the expected number of children E ξ =\nL207@P4: P∞\nL208@P4: k=0 kπk satisfies\nL209@P4: Eξ < 1, E ξ = 1 or Eξ > 1. It is a standard basic fact of branching process\nL210@P4: theory that T is finite a.s. if Eξ 6 1 (i.e., in the subcritical and critical cases),\nL211@P4: but T is infinite with positive probability if E ξ > 1 (the supercritical case),\nL212@P4-5: see e.g. Athreya and Ney [8].6 SVANTE JANSON\nL213@P5: The Galton–Watson trees have random sizes. We are mainly interested\nL214@P5: in random trees with a given size; we thus define Tn as T conditioned on\nL215@P5: |T | = n. These random trees Tn are called conditioned Galton–Watson trees.\nL216@P5: By definition, Tn has size |Tn| = n.\nL217@P5: It is well-known that several important classes of random trees can be\nL218@P5: seen as conditioned Galton–Watson tree, see e.g. Aldous [4], Devroye [32],\nL219@P5: Drmota [33] and Section 9.\nL220@P5: 2.3. Simply generated trees. The random trees that we will study are a\nL221@P5: generalization of the Galton–Watson trees. We suppose in this paper that we\nL222@P5: are given a fixed weight sequence w = (wk)k>0 of non-negative real numbers.\nL223@P5: We then define the weight of a finite tree T ∈ Tf by\nL224@P5: w(T) := Y\nL225@P5: v∈T\nL226@P5: wd+(v), (2.3)\nL227@P5: taking the product over all nodes v in T. Trees with such weights are called\nL228@P5: simply generated trees and were introduced by Meir and Moon [85]. To avoid\nL229@P5: trivialities, we assume that w0 > 0 and that there exists some k > 2 with\nL230@P5: wk > 0.\nL231@P5: We let Tn be the random tree obtained by picking an element of Tn at\nL232@P5: random with probability proportional to its weight, i.e.,\nL233@P5: P(Tn = T) = w(T)\nL234@P5: Zn\nL235@P5: , T ∈ Tn, (2.4)\nL236@P5: where the normalizing factor Zn is given by\nL237@P5: Zn = Zn(w) := X\nL238@P5: T ∈Tn\nL239@P5: w(T); (2.5)\nL240@P5: Zn is known as the partition function. This definition makes sense only\nL241@P5: when Zn > 0; we tacitly consider only such n when we discuss Tn. Our\nL242@P5: assumptions w0 > 0 and wk > 0 for some k > 2 imply that Zn > 0 for\nL243@P5: infinitely many n, see Corollary 14.6 for a more precise result. (In most\nL244@P5: applications, w1 > 0, and then Zn > 0 for every n > 1, so there is no\nL245@P5: problem at all. The archetypical example with a parity restriction is given\nL246@P5: by the random (full) binary tree, see Example 9.3, for which Zn > 0 if and\nL247@P5: only if n is odd.)\nL248@P5: One particularly important case is when P∞\nL249@P5: k=0 wk = 1, so the weight\nL250@P5: sequence (wk) is a probability distribution on Z>0. (We then say that (wk)\nL251@P5: is a probability weight sequence.) In this case we let ξ be a random variable\nL252@P5: with the corresponding distribution: P(ξ = k) = wk; we further let T be\nL253@P5: the random Galton–Watson tree generated by ξ. It follows directly from the\nL254@P5: definitions that for every finite tree T ∈ Tf, P(T = T) = w(T). Hence\nL255@P5: Zn = P(|T | = n) (2.6)\nL256@P5: and the simply generated random tree Tn is the same as the random Galton–\nL257@P5: Watson tree T conditioned on |T | = n, i.e., it equals the conditioned Galton–\nL258@P5-6: Watson tree Tn defined above.SIMPLY GENERATED TREES AND RANDOM ALLOCATIONS 7\nL259@P6: It is well-known, see Section 4 for details, that in many cases it is possible\nL260@P6: to change the weight sequence (wk) to a probability weight sequence without\nL261@P6: changing the distribution of the random trees Tn; in this case Tn can thus be\nL262@P6: seen as a conditioned Galton–Watson tree. Moreover, in many cases this can\nL263@P6: be done such that the resulting probability distribution has mean 1. In such\nL264@P6: cases it thus suffices to consider the case of a probability weight sequence\nL265@P6: with mean E ξ = 1; then Tn is a conditional critical Galton–Watson tree. It\nL266@P6: turns out that this is a nice and natural setting, with many known results\nL267@P6: proved by many different authors. (In many papers it is further assumed\nL268@P6: that ξ has finite variance, or even a finite exponential moment. This is not\nL269@P6: needed for the main results presented here, but may be necessary for other\nL270@P6: results. See also Sections 8, 18 and 20.)\nL271@P6: 3. Notation\nL272@P6: We consider a fixed weight sequence w = (wk)k>0. The support supp(w)\nL273@P6: of the weight sequence w = (wk) is {k : wk > 0}. We define\nL274@P6: ω = ω(w) := sup supp(w) = sup{k : wk > 0} 6 ∞, (3.1)\nL275@P6: (When considering Tn, we assume, as said above, w0 > 0 and wk > 0 for\nL276@P6: some k > 2; this can be written 0 ∈ supp(w) and ω > 2.)\nL277@P6: We further define (assuming that the support contains at least two points)\nL278@P6: span(w) := max{d > 1 : d | (i − j) whenever wi, wj > 0}. (3.2)\nL279@P6: Since we assume w0 > 0, i.e., 0 ∈ supp(w), we can simplify this to\nL280@P6: span(w) = max{d > 1 : d | i whenever wi > 0}, (3.3)\nL281@P6: the greatest common divisor of supp(w).\nL282@P6: We let\nL283@P6: Φ(z) := X∞\nL284@P6: k=0\nL285@P6: wkz\nL286@P6: k\nL287@P6: (3.4)\nL288@P6: be the generating function of the given weight sequence, and let ρ ∈ [0,∞]\nL289@P6: be its radius of convergence. Thus\nL290@P6: ρ = 1/ lim sup\nL291@P6: k→∞\nL292@P6: w\nL293@P6: 1/k\nL294@P6: k\nL295@P6: . (3.5)\nL296@P6: Φ(ρ) is always defined, with 0 < Φ(ρ) 6